---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
---

<Layout
  title="Raising Kids in Wartime Israel | Expert Guidance"
  description="Evidence-based tips from Israeli and international mental health experts for parents raising children amid conflict and trauma in Israel."
>

<style>
  .hero-gradient {
    background: linear-gradient(135deg, #1e1b4b 0%, #312e81 40%, #1e3a5f 100%);
  }
  .card { transition: transform 0.2s ease, box-shadow 0.2s ease; }
  .card:hover { transform: translateY(-2px); }
  .source-tag {
    font-size: 0.7rem;
    background: rgba(99,102,241,0.15);
    color: #a5b4fc;
    border: 1px solid rgba(99,102,241,0.3);
    border-radius: 9999px;
    padding: 2px 10px;
    display: inline-block;
    margin-top: 8px;
  }
  .category-badge {
    font-size: 0.7rem;
    font-weight: 700;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    letter-spacing: 0.05em;
    padding: 3px 10px;
    border-radius: 9999px;
  }
  .stat-card {
    background: rgba(255,255,255,0.05);
    border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.1);
    border-radius: 12px;
    padding: 1.25rem;
    text-align: center;
  }
</style>

<!-- Hero -->
<div class="hero-gradient text-white py-16 px-4">
  <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto text-center">
    <p class="text-indigo-300 text-sm font-semibold uppercase tracking-widest mb-3">Evidence-Based Guidance</p>
    <h1 class="text-3xl sm:text-4xl font-bold mb-4 leading-tight">
      Raising Children in Wartime Israel
    </h1>
    <p class="text-indigo-200 text-lg max-w-2xl mx-auto leading-relaxed">
      Practical, research-backed guidance from Israeli psychologists, pediatricians,
      and trauma specialists working with families since October 7, 2023.
    </p>

    <!-- Stats row -->
    <div class="grid grid-cols-3 gap-4 mt-10 max-w-xl mx-auto">
      <div class="stat-card">
        <div class="text-2xl font-bold text-white">84%</div>
        <div class="text-xs text-indigo-300 mt-1">of Israeli children show emotional distress post-Oct 7</div>
      </div>
      <div class="stat-card">
        <div class="text-2xl font-bold text-white">69%</div>
        <div class="text-xs text-indigo-300 mt-1">experience anxiety symptoms</div>
      </div>
      <div class="stat-card">
        <div class="text-2xl font-bold text-white">28%</div>
        <div class="text-xs text-indigo-300 mt-1">of parents meet PTSD clinical threshold</div>
      </div>
    </div>
    <p class="text-indigo-400 text-xs mt-3">Source: Israeli Pediatric Association &amp; Hebrew University / Yale Study, 2024–2025</p>
  </div>
</div>

<!-- Main content -->
<div class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-4 py-12 space-y-12">

  <!-- Key finding callout -->
  <div class="bg-emerald-900/30 border border-emerald-500/40 rounded-xl p-6 text-center">
    <p class="text-emerald-300 text-lg font-semibold leading-relaxed">
      "Even traumatized parents can protect their children — the way parents talk
      with children about feelings makes a real difference."
    </p>
    <p class="text-emerald-500 text-sm mt-2">
      — Dr. Mor Kleynikov, Hebrew University / Yale University,
      <em>International Journal on Child Maltreatment</em>, November 2025
    </p>
  </div>

  <!-- Section 1: Talk About It -->
  <section>
    <div class="flex items-center gap-3 mb-6">
      <div class="w-9 h-9 rounded-full bg-blue-500/20 flex items-center justify-center text-blue-400 text-lg">💬</div>
      <h2 class="text-xl font-bold text-primary-light dark:text-primary-dark">How to Talk With Your Children</h2>
    </div>

    <div class="grid sm:grid-cols-2 gap-4">

      <div class="card bg-card-bg-light dark:bg-card-bg-dark border border-border-light dark:border-border-dark rounded-xl p-5">
        <span class="category-badge bg-blue-500/20 text-blue-400">Tell the truth</span>
        <h3 class="font-semibold text-primary-light dark:text-primary-dark mt-3 mb-2">Give age-appropriate facts — without graphic details</h3>
        <p class="text-sm text-secondary-light dark:text-secondary-dark leading-relaxed">
          Children need to hear the truth from a trusted adult, not from classmates or social media.
          Use simple, honest language while leaving out graphic details. Letting children hear from you first gives
          you the chance to shape their understanding.
        </p>
        <span class="source-tag">Dr. Naomi Baum, METIV Israel Psychotrauma Center</span>
      </div>

      <div class="card bg-card-bg-light dark:bg-card-bg-dark border border-border-light dark:border-border-dark rounded-xl p-5">
        <span class="category-badge bg-purple-500/20 text-purple-400">Encourage venting</span>
        <h3 class="font-semibold text-primary-light dark:text-primary-dark mt-3 mb-2">Invite children to express their fears openly</h3>
        <p class="text-sm text-secondary-light dark:text-secondary-dark leading-relaxed">
          Actively invite children to talk about what scares or angers them rather than keeping feelings inside.
          This "venting" strategy — one of two highlighted in the Hebrew University / Yale study — significantly
          reduced children's anxiety and behavioral problems.
        </p>
        <span class="source-tag">Kleynikov et al., Int. Journal on Child Maltreatment, 2025</span>
      </div>

      <div class="card bg-card-bg-light dark:bg-card-bg-dark border border-border-light dark:border-border-dark rounded-xl p-5">
        <span class="category-badge bg-teal-500/20 text-teal-400">Acceptance</span>
        <h3 class="font-semibold text-primary-light dark:text-primary-dark mt-3 mb-2">Validate all emotions — don't rush to "fix" them</h3>
        <p class="text-sm text-secondary-light dark:text-secondary-dark leading-relaxed">
          Help children acknowledge that fear, anger, sadness, and confusion are all natural responses.
          The second key strategy in the Yale study: don't suppress or minimize feelings.
          "All feelings are legitimate, and no feelings should be dismissed or judged inappropriate."
        </p>
        <span class="source-tag">Dr. Naomi Baum, METIV; Kleynikov et al. 2025</span>
      </div>

      <div class="card bg-card-bg-light dark:bg-card-bg-dark border border-border-light dark:border-border-dark rounded-xl p-5">
        <span class="category-badge bg-orange-500/20 text-orange-400">Stay calm</span>
        <h3 class="font-semibold text-primary-light dark:text-primary-dark mt-3 mb-2">Your emotional state is contagious</h3>
        <p class="text-sm text-secondary-light dark:text-secondary-dark leading-relaxed">
          Children's responses to terror are closely tied to how surrounding adults respond.
          The younger the child, the greater the influence. Maintain a calm, loving presence
          while keeping lines of communication open for honest discussion.
        </p>
        <span class="source-tag">Dr. Naomi Baum, METIV Israel Psychotrauma Center</span>
      </div>

    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- Section 2: Safety & Routine -->
  <section>
    <div class="flex items-center gap-3 mb-6">
      <div class="w-9 h-9 rounded-full bg-amber-500/20 flex items-center justify-center text-amber-400 text-lg">🏠</div>
      <h2 class="text-xl font-bold text-primary-light dark:text-primary-dark">Safety, Routine & Reassurance</h2>
    </div>

    <div class="grid sm:grid-cols-2 gap-4">

      <div class="card bg-card-bg-light dark:bg-card-bg-dark border border-border-light dark:border-border-dark rounded-xl p-5">
        <span class="category-badge bg-amber-500/20 text-amber-400">Reassure</span>
        <h3 class="font-semibold text-primary-light dark:text-primary-dark mt-3 mb-2">Stress real protective actions being taken</h3>
        <p class="text-sm text-secondary-light dark:text-secondary-dark leading-relaxed">
          Tell children concretely what is being done to keep them safe: "The army and security forces
          are working to protect us." Emphasize real actions rather than vague reassurances —
          this helps children feel as secure as possible in an uncertain situation.
        </p>
        <span class="source-tag">Elaine Hoter, educator &amp; bereaved mother, Israel Forever Foundation</span>
      </div>

      <div class="card bg-card-bg-light dark:bg-card-bg-dark border border-border-light dark:border-border-dark rounded-xl p-5">
        <span class="category-badge bg-green-500/20 text-green-400">Normalcy</span>
        <h3 class="font-semibold text-primary-light dark:text-primary-dark mt-3 mb-2">Maintain routine as much as possible</h3>
        <p class="text-sm text-secondary-light dark:text-secondary-dark leading-relaxed">
          Predictable daily schedules — meals, school, bedtime — are a powerful stabilizer for children
          under stress. The APA recommends maintaining normalcy as a core coping strategy for families
          in conflict zones. Even small rituals provide psychological anchoring.
        </p>
        <span class="source-tag">American Psychological Association, 2025</span>
      </div>

      <div class="card bg-card-bg-light dark:bg-card-bg-dark border border-border-light dark:border-border-dark rounded-xl p-5">
        <span class="category-badge bg-red-500/20 text-red-400">Media</span>
        <h3 class="font-semibold text-primary-light dark:text-primary-dark mt-3 mb-2">Limit and monitor media exposure</h3>
        <p class="text-sm text-secondary-light dark:text-secondary-dark leading-relaxed">
          Exposure to repeated images and footage of violence significantly increases trauma symptoms
          in children. Monitor and limit news consumption, especially social media.
          Address any disturbing images children have already seen directly and calmly.
        </p>
        <span class="source-tag">APA; Dr. Zieva Dauber Konvisser, <em>Living Beyond Terrorism</em></span>
      </div>

      <div class="card bg-card-bg-light dark:bg-card-bg-dark border border-border-light dark:border-border-dark rounded-xl p-5">
        <span class="category-badge bg-pink-500/20 text-pink-400">Touch</span>
        <h3 class="font-semibold text-primary-light dark:text-primary-dark mt-3 mb-2">Physical comfort matters — hugs, holding hands</h3>
        <p class="text-sm text-secondary-light dark:text-secondary-dark leading-relaxed">
          When breaking difficult news to a child, where appropriate, physical touch — a hug or
          holding hands — provides a comforting counterbalance to frightening information.
          Have these conversations in a familiar, safe setting such as home.
        </p>
        <span class="source-tag">Dr. Zieva Dauber Konvisser; METIV Israel Psychotrauma Center</span>
      </div>

    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- Section 3: Expression & Resilience -->
  <section>
    <div class="flex items-center gap-3 mb-6">
      <div class="w-9 h-9 rounded-full bg-purple-500/20 flex items-center justify-center text-purple-400 text-lg">🎨</div>
      <h2 class="text-xl font-bold text-primary-light dark:text-primary-dark">Expression, Play & Resilience</h2>
    </div>

    <div class="grid sm:grid-cols-2 gap-4">

      <div class="card bg-card-bg-light dark:bg-card-bg-dark border border-border-light dark:border-border-dark rounded-xl p-5">
        <span class="category-badge bg-purple-500/20 text-purple-400">Expression</span>
        <h3 class="font-semibold text-primary-light dark:text-primary-dark mt-3 mb-2">Let children choose how they express feelings</h3>
        <p class="text-sm text-secondary-light dark:text-secondary-dark leading-relaxed">
          Some children talk; others draw, listen to music, use clay, paint, or physically release tension.
          As parents we often know which method works best for each child. Play and
          creative expression are protective factors against depression, anxiety, and panic.
        </p>
        <span class="source-tag">Dr. Ruth Pat-Horenczyk, METIV; Elaine Hoter, Israel Forever</span>
      </div>

      <div class="card bg-card-bg-light dark:bg-card-bg-dark border border-border-light dark:border-border-dark rounded-xl p-5">
        <span class="category-badge bg-indigo-500/20 text-indigo-400">Empower</span>
        <h3 class="font-semibold text-primary-light dark:text-primary-dark mt-3 mb-2">Give children something meaningful they can do</h3>
        <p class="text-sm text-secondary-light dark:text-secondary-dark leading-relaxed">
          One of the most effective ways to empower a child after trauma is an action they can take —
          writing letters of support to affected families, helping with community efforts, or
          raising funds. Agency counters helplessness.
        </p>
        <span class="source-tag">Dr. Zieva Dauber Konvisser; Israel Forever Foundation</span>
      </div>

      <div class="card bg-card-bg-light dark:bg-card-bg-dark border border-border-light dark:border-border-dark rounded-xl p-5">
        <span class="category-badge bg-cyan-500/20 text-cyan-400">Connection</span>
        <h3 class="font-semibold text-primary-light dark:text-primary-dark mt-3 mb-2">Foster national belonging and collective grief</h3>
        <p class="text-sm text-secondary-light dark:text-secondary-dark leading-relaxed">
          Balance empathy for victims with not overwhelming the child. Encourage children to see
          themselves as part of a resilient nation while shielding them from graphic details.
          Collective mourning and solidarity can be a source of strength, not only pain.
        </p>
        <span class="source-tag">Elaine Hoter, Israel Forever Foundation</span>
      </div>

      <div class="card bg-card-bg-light dark:bg-card-bg-dark border border-border-light dark:border-border-dark rounded-xl p-5">
        <span class="category-badge bg-green-500/20 text-green-400">Hope</span>
        <h3 class="font-semibold text-primary-light dark:text-primary-dark mt-3 mb-2">Model hope and a positive orientation to the future</h3>
        <p class="text-sm text-secondary-light dark:text-secondary-dark leading-relaxed">
          Being hopeful and keeping a positive attitude are not naïve — they are documented
          protective factors. Children who see adults maintaining forward-looking orientations
          grow into more resilient individuals even having lived through trauma.
        </p>
        <span class="source-tag">Dr. Zieva Dauber Konvisser, Israel Forever Foundation</span>
      </div>

    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- Section 4: For Traumatized Parents -->
  <section>
    <div class="flex items-center gap-3 mb-6">
      <div class="w-9 h-9 rounded-full bg-rose-500/20 flex items-center justify-center text-rose-400 text-lg">❤️</div>
      <h2 class="text-xl font-bold text-primary-light dark:text-primary-dark">If You Are Struggling Too</h2>
    </div>

    <div class="bg-rose-900/20 border border-rose-500/30 rounded-xl p-6 mb-4">
      <p class="text-rose-200 font-semibold mb-2">The landmark 2025 finding: you don't need to be okay to help your child.</p>
      <p class="text-sm text-rose-300 leading-relaxed">
        A study of 318 Israeli parents by Hebrew University, University of Haifa, Bar-Ilan University,
        and Yale University found that 28% met the clinical threshold for PTSD. Yet even parents with
        severe PTSD symptoms could protect their children by using supportive emotional strategies.
        "Parents do not need to be 'perfect' to help their children. Listening and creating space
        for emotions is what matters most."
      </p>
      <span class="source-tag">Kleynikov, Lassri et al., <em>International Journal on Child Maltreatment</em>, November 2025</span>
    </div>

    <div class="grid sm:grid-cols-3 gap-4">

      <div class="card bg-card-bg-light dark:bg-card-bg-dark border border-border-light dark:border-border-dark rounded-xl p-5">
        <h3 class="font-semibold text-primary-light dark:text-primary-dark mb-2">Take care of yourself first</h3>
        <p class="text-sm text-secondary-light dark:text-secondary-dark leading-relaxed">
          Tend to your own needs and don't face conflict-related stress alone. Your own mental health
          directly shapes your children's wellbeing. Seek peer support, professionals, and community.
        </p>
        <span class="source-tag">APA, 2025</span>
      </div>

      <div class="card bg-card-bg-light dark:bg-card-bg-dark border border-border-light dark:border-border-dark rounded-xl p-5">
        <h3 class="font-semibold text-primary-light dark:text-primary-dark mb-2">You don't need all the answers</h3>
        <p class="text-sm text-secondary-light dark:text-secondary-dark leading-relaxed">
          It's okay not to know the answer to every question. Being honest with your child and
          learning together is more valuable than projecting false certainty. Acknowledge
          shared uncertainty with warmth.
        </p>
        <span class="source-tag">Dr. Naomi Baum, METIV; Dr. Zieva Dauber Konvisser</span>
      </div>

      <div class="card bg-card-bg-light dark:bg-card-bg-dark border border-border-light dark:border-border-dark rounded-xl p-5">
        <h3 class="font-semibold text-primary-light dark:text-primary-dark mb-2">Seek professional help when needed</h3>
        <p class="text-sm text-secondary-light dark:text-secondary-dark leading-relaxed">
          Workbooks, school tools, and community groups are helpful starting points but are
          not replacements for mental health professionals. If symptoms persist, connect with
          a trained trauma therapist.
        </p>
        <span class="source-tag">David Mandel, CEO, Ohel Children's Home &amp; Family Services</span>
      </div>

    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- Age-based quick guide -->
  <section>
    <div class="flex items-center gap-3 mb-6">
      <div class="w-9 h-9 rounded-full bg-yellow-500/20 flex items-center justify-center text-yellow-400 text-lg">👶</div>
      <h2 class="text-xl font-bold text-primary-light dark:text-primary-dark">By Age Group</h2>
    </div>

    <div class="grid sm:grid-cols-3 gap-4">
      <div class="card bg-card-bg-light dark:bg-card-bg-dark border border-border-light dark:border-border-dark rounded-xl p-5">
        <div class="text-yellow-400 font-bold text-sm mb-3 uppercase tracking-wide">Under 6</div>
        <ul class="text-sm text-secondary-light dark:text-secondary-dark space-y-2 leading-relaxed">
          <li>• Keep explanations very simple and concrete</li>
          <li>• Prioritize physical closeness and routine</li>
          <li>• Use play and drawing as communication</li>
          <li>• Focus entirely on "you are safe right now"</li>
          <li>• Avoid TV news entirely</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
      <div class="card bg-card-bg-light dark:bg-card-bg-dark border border-border-light dark:border-border-dark rounded-xl p-5">
        <div class="text-orange-400 font-bold text-sm mb-3 uppercase tracking-wide">Ages 6–12</div>
        <ul class="text-sm text-secondary-light dark:text-secondary-dark space-y-2 leading-relaxed">
          <li>• Provide basic factual explanation without graphic detail</li>
          <li>• Answer questions honestly at their level</li>
          <li>• Use resilience workbooks designed for this age</li>
          <li>• Channel energy into helping others</li>
          <li>• Monitor and limit social media exposure</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
      <div class="card bg-card-bg-light dark:bg-card-bg-dark border border-border-light dark:border-border-dark rounded-xl p-5">
        <div class="text-red-400 font-bold text-sm mb-3 uppercase tracking-wide">Teenagers</div>
        <ul class="text-sm text-secondary-light dark:text-secondary-dark space-y-2 leading-relaxed">
          <li>• Engage in honest, age-equal conversations</li>
          <li>• Validate complex or conflicting emotions</li>
          <li>• Discuss media critically together</li>
          <li>• Support peer connection and community</li>
          <li>• Watch for withdrawal, aggression, or sleep changes</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </div>
    <p class="text-xs text-secondary-light dark:text-secondary-dark mt-3 text-center">
      Based on guidance from METIV, Dr. Naomi Baum, and the Binational School of Psychotherapy (PBS NewsHour, Jan 2024)
    </p>
  </section>

  <!-- Resources -->
  <section class="bg-card-bg-light dark:bg-card-bg-dark border border-border-light dark:border-border-dark rounded-xl p-6">
    <h2 class="text-lg font-bold text-primary-light dark:text-primary-dark mb-4">Expert Resources</h2>
    <div class="grid sm:grid-cols-2 gap-3 text-sm">
      <a href="https://metiv.org/trauma-information/how-to-help-children/" target="_blank" rel="noopener"
         class="flex items-start gap-2 text-indigo-400 hover:text-indigo-300 hover:underline">
        <span class="mt-0.5">↗</span>
        <span>METIV Israel Psychotrauma Center — How to Help Children</span>
      </a>
      <a href="https://www.afnatal.org/supporting-your-children-in-times-of-stress/" target="_blank" rel="noopener"
         class="flex items-start gap-2 text-indigo-400 hover:text-indigo-300 hover:underline">
        <span class="mt-0.5">↗</span>
        <span>NATAL — Supporting Your Children in Times of Stress</span>
      </a>
      <a href="https://childmind.org/article/helping-children-cope-frightening-news/" target="_blank" rel="noopener"
         class="flex items-start gap-2 text-indigo-400 hover:text-indigo-300 hover:underline">
        <span class="mt-0.5">↗</span>
        <span>Child Mind Institute — Helping Children Cope with Frightening News</span>
      </a>
      <a href="https://israelforever.org/interact/blog/how_can_parents_help_children_cope_with_trauma/" target="_blank" rel="noopener"
         class="flex items-start gap-2 text-indigo-400 hover:text-indigo-300 hover:underline">
        <span class="mt-0.5">↗</span>
        <span>Israel Forever Foundation — How Can Parents Help Children Cope With Trauma?</span>
      </a>
      <a href="https://www.apa.org/monitor/2025/10/coping-loved-ones-war-zones" target="_blank" rel="noopener"
         class="flex items-start gap-2 text-indigo-400 hover:text-indigo-300 hover:underline">
        <span class="mt-0.5">↗</span>
        <span>American Psychological Association — Coping When Loved Ones Are in War Zones</span>
      </a>
      <a href="https://israel.com/health/even-traumatized-parents-can-shield-their-children-from-wars-emotional-toll-study-finds/" target="_blank" rel="noopener"
         class="flex items-start gap-2 text-indigo-400 hover:text-indigo-300 hover:underline">
        <span class="mt-0.5">↗</span>
        <span>Israel.com — Even Traumatized Parents Can Shield Their Children (Hebrew U / Yale, 2025)</span>
      </a>
      <a href="https://www.pbs.org/newshour/show/how-mental-health-experts-are-handling-the-israel-hamas-wars-lasting-impact-on-children" target="_blank" rel="noopener"
         class="flex items-start gap-2 text-indigo-400 hover:text-indigo-300 hover:underline">
        <span class="mt-0.5">↗</span>
        <span>PBS NewsHour — Binational School of Psychotherapy on Children in Conflict</span>
      </a>
      <a href="https://metiv.org/trauma-information/how-to-help-children/9-ways-to-support-children-after-a-traumatic-event/" target="_blank" rel="noopener"
         class="flex items-start gap-2 text-indigo-400 hover:text-indigo-300 hover:underline">
        <span class="mt-0.5">↗</span>
        <span>METIV — 9 Ways to Support Children After a Traumatic Event</span>
      </a>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- Footer note -->
  <p class="text-xs text-secondary-light dark:text-secondary-dark text-center leading-relaxed pb-6">
    All guidance on this page is sourced from published research and named experts.
    This page is informational and does not substitute for professional mental health care.
    If you or your child are in crisis, contact a licensed mental health professional or NATAL's crisis line.
  </p>

</div>

</Layout>
